     Thunar's factory toolbar, arranged the way the Finder arranges its own:
     navigation, then the split-view toggle, then the three view-mode buttons
     with the active one held down, then the location bar and search. Thunar
     persists the whole toolbar in this single property - each entry is
     item:visible, IN DISPLAY ORDER - so the layout a person sees on first
     boot is one seeded line, and "Configure Toolbar..." remains the
     supported way to change it per room, persisting in the room's home like
     every other choice.

     The view-mode buttons are the point of the exercise: they are the half
     of the Finder's toolbar that GTK can honestly deliver. The half it
     cannot - Miller columns, gallery view, tags, grouping, share - is
     documented as impossible rather than half-built, and no button here
     pretends otherwise.

     Custom actions (uca-*) are deliberately absent: their identifiers are
     generated per profile at first run, so a hardcoded one would name an
     action that does not exist. Thunar appends unknown actions to the end
     on its own, hidden by default. -->
<channel name="thunar" version="1.0">
  <property name="last-toolbar-items" type="string" value="menu:0,back:1,forward:1,open-parent:1,open-home:1,new-tab:0,new-window:0,toggle-split-view:1,undo:0,redo:0,zoom-out:0,zoom-in:0,zoom-reset:0,view-as-icons:1,view-as-detailed-list:1,view-as-compact-list:1,view-switcher:0,location-bar:1,reload:0,search:1"/>
</channel>
